%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 1033564482, guid: 84bf46673498d8d4698d465c26c572a2, type: 3}
  m_Name: SampleHubCatalog
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Entries:
  - m_Id: animation-sync
    m_Title: Animation Sync
    m_Description: 'The AnimationSynchronizer component will set the Forward float
      parameter to the NavMeshAgent''s direction. This will then synchronize the
      animation with the behavior tree.


      This implementation is specific to
      the sample Animator Controller. For your own implementation you will need to
      create a new script which applies the parameters based on your Animator Controller
      structure. It does not directly interact with the behavior tree.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Animation
    m_SceneGUID: a089b50b98ce00f449737bc9b119e261
    m_PreviewGUID: 4bf2d407be5d84b4ea42b8a1934eee6b
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.ai.navigation@2.0/manual/CouplingAnimationAndNavigation.html
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: conditional-aborts
    m_Title: Conditional Aborts
    m_Description: Conditional aborts are a key feature in order to reevaluate the
      behavior tree. The Agent behavior tree uses a Lower Priority Conditional Abort
      to continuously check if the enemy is within sight. As soon as the enemy is
      in sight the abort will trigger and the agent will start to seek towards the
      enemy.
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Conditional Aborts
    m_SceneGUID: fc93009833555af49b47a2ae1d96c4eb
    m_PreviewGUID: 21cea2b1bdb2d1e42843e2b4ba7d592a
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/conditional-aborts/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: entities
    m_Title: Entities
    m_Description: 'These scene demonstrates using behavior trees exclusively within
      ECS. The behavior trees which controls each agent within the swarm as well
      as the turret''s behavior tree only contains entity tasks allowing for an large
      number of spawnable agents.


      The turret uses Unity''s baking system
      in order to spawn within the ECS system. The swarm agents are spawned at runtime
      using a prefab. See the EntitySpawner component for an example of spawning
      entity agents at runtime.'
    m_Difficulty: 2
    m_Tags:
    - DOTS
    m_SceneGUID: cc58573cb28911642bac3e057bd71caa
    m_PreviewGUID: 920b7c9b64a91fc49879c49511ca9f53
    m_DocumentationUrl: 
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.entities.graphics
      m_DisplayName: Entities Graphics
  - m_Id: event-branches
    m_Title: Event Branches
    m_Description: 'This scene demonstrates using event branches to react to various
      events. Event branches can trigger when an interrupt such as a conditional
      abort occurs, when a physics callback occurs, when an event occurs, and in
      other situations. The behavior tree Start branch must be active in order for
      other event branches to be triggered.


      In this scene event branches
      are used to cause the character to jump, crouch, add the coin amount, and reach
      the end point.'
    m_Difficulty: 0
    m_Tags:
    - Events
    m_SceneGUID: 6734ce68954fc46478cf439b751fbb51
    m_PreviewGUID: acc525a90e0b4094d87843ee7a4a4546
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/new-tasks/event-node/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
  - m_Id: events
    m_Title: Events
    m_Description: This scene gives an example of sending events within the behavior
      tree. A global event is sent when the turret projectile hits the agent in order
      to summon the medic for aid. A local event is then sent from the medic to the
      agent after aid has been given.
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Events
    m_SceneGUID: 62a229d2b640c5548ae866d2205e462a
    m_PreviewGUID: a79e1b4f249d4ce41926877cc5b84e3b
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/events
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: hide-and-seek
    m_Title: Hide And Seek
    m_Description: 'This scene gives a practical example of how you could structure
      your behavior tree to search for a player and then seek the player if they
      are seen.


      The Can See Object task is being reevaluated with the conditional
      aborts system in order for the agent to determine if the player is within sight.
      If the player is within sight the AI agent will start to chase after the player
      as long as the player is within a specified distance. This distance check is
      also reevaluated with the conditional aborts system.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Conditional Aborts
    m_SceneGUID: f3eb9475a38ea674d9b9bcbc4b052082
    m_PreviewGUID: ff023dc2bd417b4409ae439084d95b1b
    m_DocumentationUrl: 
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.inputsystem
      m_DisplayName: Input System
  - m_Id: melee-combat
    m_Title: Melee Combat
    m_Description: 'This scene uses a common behavior tree design pattern. It demonstrates
      using an alternating Selector and Sequence structure, making it easy to add
      or remove behavior without needing to restructure the behavior tree.


      The
      behavior tree agents also use a Subtree reference assigned on the BehaviorTree
      component, which allows the use of the same tree for both agents.'
    m_Difficulty: 0
    m_Tags:
    - Subtrees
    m_SceneGUID: 8cf15a2f3c5afa344843fb764936e5a0
    m_PreviewGUID: c34eaa78db39153478e7dfa1996b0612
    m_DocumentationUrl: 
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
  - m_Id: persistent-scenes-a
    m_Title: Persistent Scenes A
    m_Description: 'Marks the agent''s GameObject as DontDestroyOnLoad, allowing
      the behavior tree to stay active throughout scene changes. See PersistentScenesManager.Awake
      for the implementation.


      Ensure the PersistentScenesA and PersistentScenesB
      scenes have been added to your build settings.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Scene Management
    m_SceneGUID: ab691bb08e1509a48a13742462ca06bd
    m_PreviewGUID: 7abcd87e6bc6468468c9e8fb04574309
    m_DocumentationUrl: 
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: persistent-scenes-b
    m_Title: Persistent Scenes B
    m_Description: 'Marks the agent''s GameObject as DontDestroyOnLoad, allowing
      the behavior tree to stay active throughout scene changes. See PersistentScenesManager.Awake
      for the implementation.


      Ensure the PersistentScenesA and PersistentScenesB
      scenes have been added to your build settings.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Scene Management
    m_SceneGUID: 2b56fe8f357736d4aa4df29e4cc73029
    m_PreviewGUID: d90b721126c650348ad2d8982c6439dc
    m_DocumentationUrl: 
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: property-binding
    m_Title: Property Binding
    m_Description: 'Property bindings allow you to map a variable value to a C# property.
      This allows you to easily use that value outside of the behavior tree and you
      do not need to worry about syncing the variable value. Properties must have
      both a getter and a setter in order for a variable to be able to bind to it.


      In
      this example the character has a Health variable that maps to the Health.Value
      property.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Variables
    m_SceneGUID: 36264f19e5a586d4a988ffeb608ac423
    m_PreviewGUID: 3dda1b910cf332c4e87c4409735f01b0
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/variables/property-bindings/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
  - m_Id: runtime-behavior
    m_Title: Runtime Behavior
    m_Description: Subtrees can be changed at runtime, allowing for dynamic behavior
      based on the game state. When the subtree should be swapped out the BehaviorTree.ReevaluateSubtreeReference
      method can be called. This will call IGraphReferenceNode.EvaluateSubgraphs,
      allowing you to return a new subtree within your reference task.
    m_Difficulty: 2
    m_Tags:
    - Subtrees
    m_SceneGUID: 4a6320d00cd529f439f27aa27d205924
    m_PreviewGUID: 0a30e3e610031fa4d894abb53667b8c5
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/subtrees/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
  - m_Id: save-load
    m_Title: Save Load
    m_Description: 'The behavior tree state can be saved, allowing it to be restored
      at a later point in time. You have the option of saving to a file or getting
      the save data, allowing you to save and restore the data using your own saving
      methods.


      In this example the behavior tree data is saved along with
      other data such as the Transform position and Animator state. This data is
      then saved to a JSON file, allowing it to be restored later on.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Save Load
    m_SceneGUID: 0421619cb9d792248b87adb7225c638d
    m_PreviewGUID: ce550ac6ef4bd34478f7e65f930607fc
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/save-load/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: subtrees
    m_Title: Subtrees
    m_Description: 'Subtrees are Scriptable Objects that can be used to reuse existing
      behavior trees. The Subtree Reference task can be used to select a subtree
      at runtime, and also allows for variables to be overridden for each subtree.


      In
      this scene the same subtree is reused for both the left and right sides. A
      unique reference is passed into each subtree, allowing the agent to collect
      and craft different items using the same subtree.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Subtrees
    m_SceneGUID: e70d406c17522d54381acdef13c26c33
    m_PreviewGUID: 69504920adb490740b238207b536fe20
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/subtrees
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
  - m_Id: turn-based
    m_Title: Turn Based
    m_Description: Behavior Designer can be used with any perspective and almost
      any game type. This example shows a 2D turn based game. The behavior tree will
      only be ticked when it is the AI's turn. The behavior tree will stop executing
      after one of the agents has fired at the player. It will then wait for the
      player to make their decision before ticking again.
    m_Difficulty: 2
    m_Tags:
    - 2D
    m_SceneGUID: c00135207bac66a40941f245164d67ab
    m_PreviewGUID: 7d7a8b60d8ccaf94b99ac952ab205471
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/api/
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.2d.sprite
      m_DisplayName: 2D Sprite
  - m_Id: utility-theory
    m_Title: Utility Theory
    m_Description: 'Utility theory is an AI technique that evaluates which action
      to perform based on a curve value. Behavior Designer Pro brings utility theory
      to behavior trees with the Utility Selector task.


      The Utility Selector
      task will evaluate the curves every tick to determine which action should be
      performed based on the highest utility value.'
    m_Difficulty: 1
    m_Tags:
    - Utility
    m_SceneGUID: fd0826ca3a40e5840b71fa33c8e4dfda
    m_PreviewGUID: a9cfaa42c16da5149833a202ad67582f
    m_DocumentationUrl: https://opsive.com/support/documentation/behavior-designer-pro/concepts/tasks/composite/utility-selector
    m_Dependencies:
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.render-pipelines.universal
      m_DisplayName: Universal RP
    - m_PackageName: com.unity.ai.navigation
      m_DisplayName: AI Navigation
